  <name><text>Ch03_P02_Ex4_Square_at_least_x</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Conjecture: for all real numbers \(x\), \(x^2\ge x\).</p>
<p>Give a counterexample. Type fractions as 1/2.</p>
<div>\(x=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A counterexample is a real number \(x\) for which \(x^2\ge x\) is false, that is \(x^2\lt x\).</p>
<p>Take \(x=\dfrac{1}{2}\). Then \(x^2=\dfrac{1}{4}\), and \(\dfrac{1}{4}\lt \dfrac{1}{2}\), so \(x^2\ge x\) is false when \(x=\dfrac{1}{2}\). The conjecture is false.</p>
<p>Which values work? Factorising, \(x^2-x=x(x-1)\), which is negative exactly when \(0\lt x\lt 1\). So the counterexamples are exactly the numbers strictly between \(0\) and \(1\). At \(x=0\) and \(x=1\) the two sides are equal, which \(\ge\) allows; for negative \(x\), \(x^2\) is positive and \(x\) is negative; and for \(x\gt 1\), squaring makes \(x\) bigger.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
